        "math_academy_id": "q-48344",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/09d006953b4fc1d111a84e78fcc6b2038d53768fb82d6f61a0b2a1d0126bbb40.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0]$\n- $x\\in [-3,2]$\n- $x\\in (0,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.",
        "worked_solution": "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,0]$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-3,2].$ It is continuous on $(-3,2),$ and also right continuous at $x=-3$ and left continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(0,\\infty )$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
